EXTENSION "ArM5_Mythic_Europe" {
    NAME "Ars Magica Fifth Edition - Mythic Europe (game use)"
    VERSION "0.4"
    SPEC_VERSION "0.4"
    RELEASE_DATE "2026-07-24"
    DEPENDS_ON "ArM5_Core_Base"

    #tK9TLedNqBSOeSYemDHTn83g ^"Game Use: Doctrine" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Core Rules"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "The source presents this as a sidebar. The deadly sins and theological virtues make excellent Personality Traits for medieval-themed characters. Beyond that, the issues covered by this section are rarely likely to come into play in sagas, which is probably as it should be."
            MENTIONS {
                "Personality Traits" -> ^"Personality Traits"
            }
        }
        #tFKYGu8a34cZkAhMtj2FODmi ^"Magic and Sin" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Core Rules"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "The source presents this as a sidebar offering an optional setting decision. One question that is not decided by the core rules of Ars Magica is whether magic is sinful in itself. It is clear that it can be used for sinful purposes, and lends itself well to all seven deadly sins, but it can also be used for good ends. If magic is inherently sinful, casting a spell is sinful no matter what the purpose. The choice you make here affects the possible relationships between the magi and the Divine. If magic is inherently sinful, saints and angels will always encourage magi to abandon it. This creates a strong tension, and so works best if interactions with the Church are rare. It does not mean that the Divine is always hostile, as the world is full of sinners, and magic, in itself, is not necessarily a particularly serious sin. If magic is not inherently sinful, magi could have close, and good, relations with the Church, saints, and angels, with occasional concerns over sins the magi committed using magic. In this case, giving up magic, whether temporarily or permanently, is a suitable penance in itself; giving up a sin can never be a penance, because it is required anyway."
        }

    #tZLYTfncOXTbHC2Oxzp59T4J ^"The Church" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "The Church is probably the quickest and easiest way to give medieval flavor to your saga. Christianity influenced every aspect of life in medieval Europe in a way that's hard to even imagine today, and does not feature in most fantasy settings. Almost everybody, including most Hermetic magi, is a Christian, and the overwhelming majority of Christians believe that the Church is essential to their religion. Unless you change the game mechanics, the Church is backed by the Divine, which puts limits on how corrupt it can be."
        }
        #tUK9hQDJrJSXEo74DK9qwwEN ^"Doctrine" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "There is one God, eternal and unchanging, who created the world from nothing and sustains it from moment to moment. This God is three persons, the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit. The Son became a human being as Jesus Christ, an event called the \"Incarnation,\" which is considered the most important event in history. Jesus Christ died on the cross, the Crucifixion, and by his death paid the penalty for all the sins committed by human beings. On the third day, he rose from the dead, in the Resurrection, and forty days later he ascended to heaven. In the beginning, God created two humans, Adam and Eve, and placed them in the Garden of Eden. There, they were tempted by Satan and sinned, and this Original Sin tainted all of their descendants. Baptism washes away that stain, and fits a person to receive God's grace. The seven deadly sins are Avarice, Envy, Greed, Lust, Pride, Sloth, and Wrath. The three theological virtues are Faith, Hope, and Love, and the greatest of these is Love. All humans have an immortal soul, created at some point before birth. After death, the soul is judged by God. Those who die perfect pass directly into heaven; those who die penitent but not yet perfect pass to purgatory; those who die impenitent are condemned to hell for eternity."
        }
        #tJ6oHCKmjnDekc6mQ6leUxu6 ^"Sacraments" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "The life of the Church is structured around the seven sacraments. Most of these mark important stages of life, but the most important of the sacraments, the Mass, is devoted solely to man's relationship with God. The seven sacraments are Baptism, Confirmation, Marriage, Extreme Unction, Ordination, Penance, and the Mass."
            MENTIONS {
                "Extreme Unction" -> ^"Extreme Unction"
            }
        }
        #tSZueWN0o0fVYAfBCVZ8yyl1 ^"Baptism" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Baptism is the first of the sacraments. It involves literally or symbolically immersing someone in water, while announcing that you are baptizing them in the name of the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit. Baptism washes away the stain of original sin, and is generally essential for salvation. Baptism can be performed by anyone, even a non-Christian, and even someone who gets the process wrong, as long as they intend to be performing a Christian baptism. In 1220 there is theological uncertainty as to the fate of babies who die unbaptized, so the Church has many procedures in place to ensure that children are baptized as soon as possible after birth. Baptism should be performed by a priest if at all possible, but in an emergency anyone may do it. As a result, Jews are just about the only unbaptized people in Mythic Europe."
            MENTIONS {
                "Mythic Europe" -> ^"Mythic Europe"
            }
        }
        #trRKXBLq75AOGncjfU3UN7fA ^"Confirmation" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Confirmation marks a person's desire to be united with the Church on earth, when he reaffirms the promises made on his behalf at baptism. This is supposed to take place at the age of reason, seven years old, but often does not. Confirmation can only be administered by a bishop or a priest specially designated as his representative, and, ideally, only after examining the candidate to ensure that he understands the Christian faith. These requirements make it hard for most medieval people to get a proper confirmation, so there are a substantial number of people who have never been confirmed. In particular, most Christian magi have not been, unless they or their parens are particularly pious."
        }
        #tPrvMm9U5x6zcqLTI8TyJ6oh ^"Marriage" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Marriage marks the start of a family, and is another sacrament that doesn't need any Church representative. A man and a woman marry by declaring that they are married to each other, as long as they are both old enough (over twelve for a woman, fourteen for a man), neither is already married, and they are not too closely related. Marriage is for life, and cannot be dissolved. The Pope occasionally issues annulments, but these merely recognize that the marriage never actually took place. Although no ceremony is required for marriage, the Church strongly encourages people to have a public ceremony involving a priest."
        }
        #tFt8ZF1E5I8TS0BVnBM3hOah ^"Extreme Unction" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Extreme Unction is the final sacrament, given just before death. While it is generally believed to ease the passage to heaven, many people believe that, if you have received it, you are dead to the world in some sense, and must enter a monastery if you recover. Similarly, people are often reluctant to receive it, as that means admitting that they are about to die, and Church doctrine is absolutely clear that it is not necessary for salvation. Extreme Unction can only be administered by a priest."
        }
        #tgScLIABxLM56oU7hvpcHzc4 ^"Ordination" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Ordination is the sacrament that turns a man into a priest, or other member of orders. There are two main classes of orders: minor orders, doorkeeper, lector, exorcist, and acolyte, and major, or holy, orders, sub-deacon, deacon, priest, and bishop. Men in holy orders may not marry, or have a living wife, whereas men in minor orders are much less restricted. All ordained men take the tonsure, which involves shaving the crown of the head, leaving a ring of hair."
        }
        #taSalZtoFPknXCMa2NqrhaHr ^"Penance" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Penance is the sacrament that responds to sin. Christians are required to confess all their sins to a priest, who then assigns a penance if he is convinced that the sinner has truly repented. The penance is something of some difficulty, which the penitent sinner performs to show that his repentance is genuine. It does not purchase forgiveness; that was bought by Christ's death. Instead, it allows the penitent to renew his respect for God in a concrete way. Common penances involve saying a certain number of prayers or psalms, giving charity or helping the poor in some other way, or going on a pilgrimage to a shrine, at a distance depending on the severity of the sin. Priests are not allowed to mention what they hear in confession to anyone, not even their superiors in the Church. The contents of confession are between the priest, the penitent, and God."
        }
        #tBBGs0uf7R63weVnVydBUewY ^"The Mass" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "In the Mass, God Himself becomes manifest in bread and wine, which is shared with the congregation. In most of Europe, the laity now only receive the bread, but in a few places the custom of receiving both bread and wine persists. The bread becomes the body of Christ, known as the \"Host,\" and the wine becomes His Blood, so that the Mass symbolizes the sacrifice of Christ for the salvation of all believers, and the participation of all believers in that sacrifice. Actually receiving the bread is known as \"taking communion,\" and a person must confess and do penance before doing so. (For large penances, a sincere intent to complete it suffices.) As a result, most people only take communion once a year, at Easter, although they attend the service most weeks. Because the consecrated bread and wine are extremely holy, they must be consumed completely during the service, with great care taken not to spill or drop any."
        }
        #tyuWIK03IIuiuYAd2rPgc1ZE ^"Game Use: Sacraments" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "The source presents this as a sidebar. The sacraments are as central a part of modern Catholicism as they were of the medieval Church, so a degree of care should be taken to avoid offending people, but they are a central part of medieval life and make good stories. Story seeds: A number of covenfolk decide that they want to be confirmed, which means taking them to the bishop, and getting them through the catechism without painting the covenant as a hive of heretics. A moderately pious magus decides to confess and take communion after several lapsed years; the confessor assigns a pilgrimage to a distant shrine, without using magic at all, as a penance. The covenfolk find out that Hermetic magic, or any other magic, is completely incapable of affecting the Host in any way, and form a fraternity devoted to honoring the Host as God's presence on earth. Someone born at the covenant becomes deeply pious, but some power seems to be driving him out of churches, and he has ominous warning dreams; it turns out that he was never baptized. He dies without working out the problem, and after his death a close friend has visions revealing that he is damned to hell, launching a quest for a way to baptize the character posthumously."
            MENTIONS {
                "Hermetic magic" -> ^"Hermetic magic"
            }
        }
        #tnpwbtPlVXdAitOXA5lWEToq ^"Saints" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Saints are people who served God faithfully when alive, and have now died and joined the Church Triumphant in heaven. (Living Christians are often called the \"Church Militant.\") Strictly speaking, angels are not saints, but the archangels Michael and Gabriel are often treated as saints. Saints are willing to intercede with God on behalf of the living, and while they have no power of their own, God is willing to listen to them. The most important saint is the Virgin Mary, followed by the twelve Apostles, particularly St Peter, and St Paul. There are many other saints, including the saints of the Old Testament, martyrs, and witnesses. The Church is, in 1220, moving towards an official list of recognized saints, although the process is not yet complete. Saints often take an interest in a particular area of life or institution, and petitions addressed to the saint tend to be more effective in that area. Saints are particularly attentive to their relics, physical objects that were closely associated with the saint in life, most often bodies or body parts."
            MENTIONS {
                "The Church" -> ^"The Church"
            }
        }
        #tqkFGzr4ZjirbLRvWgm0JbGi ^"Game Use: Saints" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "The source presents this as a sidebar. Saints are an excellent medium for divine intervention in a saga. The attitude of saints to the covenant and the magi depends on whether you decide that magic is inherently sinful. It need not be; magic is opposed by both Divine and Infernal auras. If magic is inherently sinful, any saint will want the magi to abandon it; if not, saints will merely urge the magi to use their power in the service of God. Story seeds: A saint takes an interest in the covenant and starts looking after it, appearing in the dreams of covenfolk offering warnings — a useful ally, but the magi will want to avoid a Dominion aura springing up within the covenant. The covenant comes into conflict, over land or some other resource, with a monastery that has a particularly protective patron saint, and their magic is opposed by the power of the Divine. Members of the covenant become particularly devoted to a saint with a distant shrine, and pilgrimages become a feature of normal life. The covenant discovers an important relic; keeping it would guarantee a stream of pilgrims and an eventual Dominion aura, but giving it away would make a firm ally."
        }
        #tUzt5izdJBmjD218YeBIeE1s ^"Structures" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "The head of the Church on earth is the Pope, the Bishop of Rome, who is the Vicar of Christ. Lower-ranked churchmen have a great deal of independence; anyone who disagrees with a lesser bishop's decision can appeal to the Pope, but in practice this is so expensive and difficult that only the most serious problems actually make it there. There are many other bishops across Europe, all based in a city. The church where the bishop has his seat is known as the \"cathedral church,\" from the Latin term for the physical chair (cathedra), and the area around is called the \"diocese.\" A bishop carries out his duties primarily by means of visitations. Within the diocese, Europe is divided up into parishes. Each parish has a church, the parish church, and a priest, the parish priest, assigned to look after the spiritual well-being of all those within, a position called \"having cure of souls.\" In some parishes, the priest with primary responsibility, called the \"rector,\" performs the duties; in others, he pays another priest, called the \"curate,\" to do the actual work."
        }
        #tId4ZdXmE6AmbKeCtno2ThSL ^"Game Use: Structures" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "The source presents this as a sidebar. All covenants are in a parish, even if they want nothing to do with the Church. Thus, the parish priest could come calling at any time, seeking out the lost sheep of his flock. Story seeds: A covenant with a cozy relationship with the parish priest hears that the bishop is coming on a visitation, which needs some response. The covenant wants to get itself recognized as a separate parish, where the magi can choose the parish priest; most covenants are large enough and rich enough to justify this, but the characters will have to negotiate a great deal of Church politics."
        }
        #tgtrTnk1qlmrJd3fEICUgsPb ^"Canon Law" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "The Church has its own law, canon law, which is based on the old Roman law but covers all aspects of Church life. It also covers marriage, inheritance, and commerce. All canon law cases must, in theory, be heard by the bishop of the appropriate diocese. Heresy is a growing concern of the church courts. Most heretics are encouraged to accept the teachings of the Church by their parish priests. Those who reach court generally recant if convicted, which always ends the case. Those who persist may finally be handed over to the secular authorities for execution. Canon law on commerce makes it illegal to lend money at interest, to buy things and sell them at a profit unless you have transported them a long distance, and to buy large stocks of something when it is cheap, to sell when it is expensive. Canon law only covers Christians, so these activities are all legal for Jews."
            MENTIONS {
                "The Church" -> ^"The Church"
            }
        }
        #tFVG9BXRMjHcJ1ooCjmxEdVl ^"Game Use: Canon Law" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "The source presents this as a sidebar. Story seeds: The magi are accused of heresy, and forced to defend themselves in court. The magi use magic to get around some of the canon law rules on commerce, making a large amount of money, but stirring up opposition from mundane merchants, who appeal to the bishop. A friendly bishop is faced with a case of heresy where the heretics appear to have diabolical powers; knowing his limits, he appoints one of the magi as his judge-delegate, sending along a learned clerk to help out, and the magus has strict instructions to save as many heretics as possible."
        }
        #tUcefowTidBLwKrDMp7M40Lo ^"Resources" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "The Church, as an institution, has extensive resources. The most basic of these is the tithe; the Church is entitled to one tenth of everything of which God gives the increase — essentially, all agricultural products. Failing to pay the tithe is a serious sin, for which heavy penances are routinely assigned. In addition, the Church owns a good deal of property, from which it draws income just as any other mundane lord, so many high-ranking clerics are personally very wealthy. In theory, one third of the tithe is supposed to be used to support the poor. In practice, much less than this is devoted to charity, but the Church is still effectively the only source of support for the most vulnerable members of society."
            MENTIONS {
                "The Church" -> ^"The Church"
            }
        }
        #t1ruja2Wb169XPO8ct0aEfhG ^"Game Use: Resources" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "The source presents this as a sidebar. Story seeds: Magi still have to pay the tithe. God gives the increase on vis sources; most priests won't care, but what if the local priest does? A covenant might be located so that the nearest major lord is a bishop or monastery, rather than a mundane noble. Alternatively, the mundane noble the covenant had good relations with might die without an heir, leave his lands to the Church, and give the covenant a new, powerful neighbor to handle."
        }
        #tZqfvYPynxgcQZadWWYefhIt ^"Monasteries" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Monasteries, and the female equivalent, convents, are a vital part of the Mythic European landscape. The members of monasteries are referred to as \"regular clergy,\" because they are bound by a rule (regulus), as opposed to the \"secular clergy,\" who live in the world (seculum). All monks and nuns take vows of poverty, chastity, stability, and obedience. The vow of poverty means that they have no personal possessions, that of chastity that they have no sexual relations, that of stability that they remain at one monastery, and that of obedience that they do whatever the head of their house commands. The monastic day is structured around the seven monastic offices, church services that take place spread throughout the day. Most monasteries are required to offer hospitality to travelers, at no charge, although visitors are expected to make a donation in line with their means. In the early years of the thirteenth century the mendicant friars, \"begging brothers,\" appear, who take vows of poverty, chastity, and obedience, and travel the world preaching and serving God. The two major orders are Franciscan and Dominican."
        }
        #tIRrJdh4cTJnvKOqjJIldcpy ^"Game Use: Monasteries" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "The source presents this as a sidebar. Story seeds: While monks make poor player characters, since they are supposed to stay in their monasteries, friars are an excellent choice, as they are supposed to wander around and thus have a perfect excuse for ending up at the covenant. Monasteries are a good place for traveling magi to stay; their duty of hospitality means that they will usually not simply turn away even those with the Blatant Gift. Monastic scholars make good correspondents for magi, and good potential allies."
            MENTIONS {
                "Blatant Gift" -> ^"Blatant Gift"
            }
        }
        #tMGQjtV1k1wGrt7pGudDh2Gb ^"Miracles" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "God intervenes in the world, and his interventions take the form of miracles. Miracles are not common events, but neither are they incredibly rare; more people have witnessed miracles than have witnessed Hermetic magic. By far the most common kind of miracle is a healing miracle, including the raising of the dead. The dead are normally raised within a week or so of dying, although God is capable of raising those who have been dead for longer if he so wishes. The next most common miracle is a punishment miracle, inflicting some sort of infirmity on a person who has been particularly disrespectful to God or his agents; these are very rarely fatal. Finally, there is a vast range of miscellaneous miracles. Most healing miracles happen at a shrine holding relics. A miracle is more likely to be granted if it is sincerely requested by a pious person, if the cause of the affliction was entirely innocent, and if an appropriate saint adds his prayers to those of the living Christian (or Jew, or Muslim). Still, God is perfectly free, and sometimes grants miracles to help people who seem less than pious."
            MENTIONS {
                "Hermetic magic" -> ^"Hermetic magic"
            }
        }
        #tLeQwjBw9H9qfPHvbBAfKV68 ^"Game Use: Miracles" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "The source presents this as a sidebar. Miracles are an excellent way to emphasize the Myth in Mythic Europe, but they are also the ultimate plot device, so storyguides should use them in moderation. Setting the frequency of miracles is an important part of deciding just how Mythic your Mythic Europe is; the frequency described in the main text is a default level, but you could make miracles very rare, maybe one per year at major shrines, or very common, so that every parish sees a handful of miracles within the parish every year. Story seeds: A grog killed in battle with a dragon is raised from the dead at the prayers of his wife and five small children, coming back with a chilling tale of purgatory and of having seen a recently deceased magus burning in hell. A magus confronting some bandits is suddenly deprived of his magical power, yet his grogs easily defeat the attackers and the magus is unharmed; his power returns afterwards. A character with a serious problem finds that the magi cannot help, as it is part of her Essential Nature, and instead seeks a miracle."
            MENTIONS {
                "Mythic Europe" -> ^"Mythic Europe"
                "Mythic Europe" -> ^"Mythic Europe"
            }
        }

    #tRNPD0oGvmPDlHp5EMq9ABUL ^"Feudalism" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Most of Europe is ruled through the feudal system, tied together by oaths of fealty in which one person swears to provide service (overwhelmingly military) for another in return for control of an area of land. The person swearing is the \"vassal,\" who promises to serve his lord in battle for a certain number of days every year; the area of land is a \"fief.\" If the fief is large, the vassal may provide the services of more than one warrior, and it is common to grant parts of a fief to other warriors on condition of service. The system resembles a pyramid with the king at the top and lords of progressively smaller fiefs below, but is far more complex: lords have direct vassals with fiefs of wildly differing sizes, vassals can swear fealty to several lords, and a lord may be a king's direct vassal for some land and a vassal of a vassal for others. Within his fief a lord is responsible for some aspects of law and order; petty crime is under the local lord's jurisdiction, and the death penalty can only be imposed by the king or some other extremely powerful nobles. All land in the fief is under the lord's control: some he farms himself (the demense), the rest he rents to peasants in return for a share of their crop and some labor on his own lands."
        }
        #tqjEXrfhrfDKevwZHmnIdMVc ^"Game Use: Feudalism" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Presented in the source as a sidebar. Because magi are required by the Code to avoid swearing fealty to any mundane lord, feudalism is more likely to be a part of the background of the saga, rather than an immediate source of story seeds. Still, the complex webs of loyalty it creates are ideal breeding grounds for stories."
        }
        #tWXvhn63F6CLtpQtXVEtJlAp ^"Travel (Nobility)" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Medieval nobles travel a lot: to fulfill the obligation to fight (wars seldom take place within a noble's own fief), to attend councils or consultations summoned by their lord or the king, and to go on pilgrimages. Lords with scattered fiefs normally travel between them over the course of the year, because it is easier to transport the noble's household than to transport enough food and drink to satisfy dozens of people for weeks. Lords also visit their vassals to reinforce bonds of loyalty, to check the land is well treated, and to weaken threatening vassals (who must expensively support their lord and entourage). Nobles also simply enjoy visiting one another."
        }
        #toplksBTkgugeUuzK8oUxrxi ^"Game Use: Travel" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Presented in the source as a sidebar. Travel allows the storyguide to bring nobles into the saga for a single session, or take them out for a period of time. Seeds: The local lord, on excellent terms with the covenant, expects a visit from his lord and asks the covenant for help defraying expenses and impressing the visitor. The local lord is powerful and only visits that fief every couple of years; when he comes the magi must tread carefully, as he doesn't know the truth about the covenant. The local lord, somewhat hostile, is called away to a great council that drags on; his wife, left behind, struggles with a problem facing the fief and calls on the magi for help. If they succeed she becomes more friendly, but her husband's attitude does not necessarily change."
        }
        #tjOmIqxidDZHec6qlJVARxFI ^"Entertainments" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "The nobility of Mythic Europe have significant leisure time, particularly when there isn't a war on, and their favored warlike pastimes include the tournament—an organized battle where participants try not to kill each other, in which winners may claim the armor and weapons of the losers. Many kings are suspicious of tournaments and most churchmen believe them inherently sinful, and they are difficult and expensive to organize and occasionally banned. The hunt is another extremely popular pastime; almost any animal can be hunted, the deer being the most popular quarry, and hunting with falcons is considered suitable for women. Some nobles even hunt magical creatures, though this is recognized as extremely dangerous. Large areas of some countries, notably England, are set aside as hunting ranges for the nobility, where no one else may hunt or cultivate; surrounding peasants have very limited activities such as foraging pigs and gathering acorns. Nobles also enjoy romances and legends (read aloud to the illiterate), entertainers such as minstrels and acrobats and trained animals, and animal fights."
            MENTIONS {
                "Mythic Europe" -> ^"Mythic Europe"
            }
        }
        #tJNkfR3K1VvwnwcjvlgCCmY9 ^"Game Use: Entertainments" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Presented in the source as a sidebar. Seeds: While hunting a magical beast, a noble party is badly injured and stumbles into the covenant seeking shelter and assistance. A group of nobles target an intelligent magical beast allied with the covenant, and the beast asks for help. A shapeshifter who can turn into a bear is getting into noble households as a \"trained bear\"; the bear is impressive, but the shapeshifter has deeper, more devious plans."
        }
        #tGYHZIR2pg0w2rPvAsRYDAhr ^"Inheritance" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Most noble power in Mythic Europe is inherited by the son from the father, though this is far from universal and succession squabbles are common, particularly over a major lordship (an \"honor\"). Younger sons present a problem: splitting the honor weakens the family, while giving them nothing stores up political trouble, so families would in one sense like exactly one son—but that is risky since people die unexpectedly, so noble families try for many children to secure a male heir. Daughters can be married to other nobles to build alliances, while younger sons must be found a place, often in the Church or in service to a more powerful lord. The most powerful lords create honors for their younger sons from holdings reverted to the king after treason or a failed line. Women only inherit if they have no brothers, and are expected to marry someone who can be the \"real\" lord; almost the only way for a woman to gain real power is as the widow of a lord who had no heirs, in which case the lands normally stay with her until her death before reverting to the overlord."
            MENTIONS {
                "Mythic Europe" -> ^"Mythic Europe"
            }
        }
        #tpfouZBJqrowpVTGVUdhy69v ^"Game Use: Inheritance" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Presented in the source as a sidebar. Seeds: A magus was the fifth son of a powerful noble; now his four older brothers have died and he is expected to become the new lord. The Order forbids it, but if he doesn't take the role, war threatens. A lord with no heirs comes to the magi for help siring a son—not difficult for magi with the appropriate Arts, but the consequences may be profound. A lord with no male heirs raised his youngest daughter as a boy to provide an heir; he died when she was very young, but the family servants kept up the pretense, so the girl is now lord of the honor—yet she is expected to marry a woman, and she seeks help from the magi."
        }
        #tass2PbLEM3Th7fxbNkRMnsB ^"Mythic Options (Nobility)" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Options for increasing the amount of magic in the nobility; use none to keep society mundane, or all to make magic everywhere. Faerie nobles might swear fealty to the king and be part of the normal feudal structure. Lesser (non-Hermetic) magicians may serve mundane nobles; a noble line where a useful supernatural ability passes between generations might hold land on condition of serving the king with that ability, and a female inheritor gains it in her own right. A line of intelligent, magical horses (or hunting dogs or hawks) might be the real rulers of a fief behind a nominal human lord. A powerful faerie or magical creature might accept the fealty of human lords while refusing to swear fealty to any human, effectively creating a new kingdom where the Code's provisions are unclear (if an area is ruled by a dragon, is it mundane?). A line of successful lords might owe their success to an enchanted item—Hermetic, another magical source, faerie, relic, or infernal—that supports incompetents or gives a competent lord an edge. An evil lord might really have sold his soul to the devil, gaining infernal powers to oppress the peasants."
        }
        #tYD1Py8GVatm8fqz970BGBFn ^"Towns and Trade" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "A town is a settlement where a significant proportion of the population (a third and up) does not make its living directly from agriculture. On this definition, towns range from villages of a few hundred inhabitants to the great metropolis of Constantinople, with tens of thousands at the very least. These points can be used to create towns that feel medieval even if they don't fit exactly into any actual part of medieval Europe."
        }
        #tCDkBdPSD3ySfzDuYx39ucqP ^"Markets and Fairs" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Markets are the stage before a town and often the nucleus around which new towns grow. A market is a formally established place and time for trading locally produced goods; most gather once per week and draw people from an area about twelve miles across, allowing people to walk to market, do business, and walk home in a single day. There may be rules about who may buy and sell what, fees for setting up stalls, and fines collected by the controlling lord at a market court. The bulk of trade is agricultural produce, with limited other goods (simple tools, clothes, home utensils) tailored to peasant customers—truly exotic goods cannot be found there. Markets are also centers for news, entertainment, and employment. Fairs are essentially temporary towns, normally happening once per year and lasting several days; the smallest draw from a large region and the largest, in the Champagne region of France, draw from the whole of Europe. A fair is like a really big market where almost anything can be bought, including luxury goods for noble and high-church customers. By the early thirteenth century the greatest days of the fairs are over as towns take over their role, but they still have much life left."
        }
        #tIHbr8NC9fwKKjpp0FViyrtC ^"Game Usage: Markets and Fairs" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Presented in the source as a sidebar. Seeds: Sending the grogs to market makes an excellent story that need not involve any magi. Markets can provide substantial income, so a covenant might establish one under its control—also a good way to slowly put magically created goods into circulation without raising suspicions—though markets bring over-curious visitors and attract hostility from competing local markets. An ambitious covenant might try to sponsor a fair, which just makes the potential problems larger. Magi needing exotic laboratory equipment or materials from distant lands travel to a fair to find them."
        }
        #tzgni54xpg8IDjy782Pp8Dq3 ^"Towns" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Most medieval towns cover a fairly small area, and many are walled; within the walls buildings can have as many as four stories and streets are very narrow. Rubbish, including from slaughterhouses, is thrown into the streets where feral animals feed off it, and sewage may be thrown into the streets or the river. The resulting bad air often unbalances the humors of those living there, so people die significantly younger in towns than in the countryside, and most towns only maintain their population through immigration. People immigrate because there are jobs: daughters of overlarge peasant families work as domestic servants, and landless peasants work as laborers or find a trade. Larger towns are becoming permanent fairs where the wealthy can buy almost anything. Towns mostly stand a little outside the feudal system, often run by councils of leading merchants or wealthy citizens rather than a warrior noble, and sometimes entirely independent and themselves lords of surrounding countryside; people may flee to a town to escape harsh lords or justice, as towns have crowds large enough to lose oneself in."
        }
        #tDUK1M2z9sETKoK2Gxdavhdc ^"Game Use: Towns" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Presented in the source as a sidebar. Clever use of magic can offset the penalties of the bad air of towns, and small magical auras, or even regiones, might persist, allowing a covenant to be sited there. Most of the story ideas for markets and fairs also apply to towns. In some countries, towns have a great deal of discretion to run their own affairs as long as they pay a certain amount of tax, so a covenant might try to be legally recognized as a town—many covenants are certainly large enough."
        }
        #th2pK98SAiMsT9scHbvMbxnk ^"Mythic Options (Towns)" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "A faerie town is just as possible as a faerie lord, and would probably be a very popular place for magi to go shopping; it might also be fully integrated into the local economy and even have some human immigrants. People with supernatural abilities could set up as traders in a town as well as in service to a lord, though this needs handling with care as towns with Magic Shops feel very much like generic fantasy. A town might be alive as a whole and aligned to any realm; people living there might be real or merely part of the town, and such a town might even be able to move."
        }
        #tOvFWuP9x5nynKyWDLxfuncE ^"Peasants" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Peasants make up 90% of medieval society. While they don't eat mud, they mostly don't eat very well, and famine is a recurrent feature of society; life in medieval Europe is much like life in the poorer parts of the contemporary Third World, only without any foreign aid. Peasants make their living from the land or sea—arable crops, livestock, or fishing—and their legal rights vary greatly from region to region, from theoretically the same as a nobleman to almost none; in all areas they are almost powerless and often cannot claim the rights they have. Most peasant families are very nearly self-sufficient, but are as curious as anyone: all have been to market, the overwhelming majority to town, and most on a pilgrimage to a local shrine. Though all illiterate and without formal education, they are neither stupid nor completely uninformed. In Mythic Europe the supernatural is part of most peasants' lives—minor magical creatures and faeries visit villages, live in crop fields, or watch over foraging pigs—but most such touches have little effect on daily life."
            MENTIONS {
                "Mythic Europe" -> ^"Mythic Europe"
            }
        }
        #tcL2A3Ev8oFkmeA7BUs6yeQX ^"Errors and Anachronisms" DEF {
            APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
            PROPERTIES {
                ^"Source" STRING "Ars Magica Fifth Edition"
            }
            DESCRIPTION "Historical errors are inevitable in any historical game. An anachronism is only a problem if it spoils the illusion that the game takes place in a particular historical period—guns and wristwatches are bound to be a problem, while getting the cut of men's tunics wrong is only a problem if a player specializes in medieval costume. Players who know more about the period can be used as a resource to explain details; a player who uses knowledge to break the mood is a problem to talk to. If reading more history reveals that the saga contradicts historical fact and it bothers you, minor details can simply be changed to be historically accurate (e.g., renaming the King of France from Louis to Philip). For things which have played an important role and are too important to change, the best option is to explicitly move your saga to an alternate history and work out a story for the discrepancy. Remember that historically there were no magi and no Order of Hermes, so the game is set in an alternate history in the first place."
            MENTIONS {
                "Order of Hermes" -> ^"Order of Hermes"
            }
        }}
